import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.2a — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your turret knows when you are near; now it needs teeth. Spawning and firing a projectile
    looks complex, but it breaks down into four smaller sub-problems:
    <strong>spawn a prefab → point it at the player → move it through space → destroy it when done</strong>.
    Today, you read Unity's projectile functions and build <code>Projectile.cs</code> from scratch.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Firing a projectile requires coordinating several distinct Unity functions.
        Read how the engine handles each step:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Sub-Problem</th><th>Unity Function</th><th>Documentation &amp; Purpose</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>1. Spawning</strong></td>
            <td><code>Instantiate(prefab, pos, rot)</code></td>
            <td>Creates a brand-new clone of a prefab at runtime and places it into the scene hierarchy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Aiming</strong></td>
            <td><code>(target - origin).normalized</code></td>
            <td>Vector subtraction produces a vector pointing toward the target. <code>.normalized</code> reduces its length to 1.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>3. Moving</strong></td>
            <td><code>transform.Translate</code> or <code>Rigidbody2D</code></td>
            <td>Moves the bullet every frame along its forward direction.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>4. Cleanup</strong></td>
            <td><code>Destroy(gameObject, delay)</code></td>
            <td>Destroys the projectile after a time delay (seconds) to prevent off-screen bullets from clogging memory.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Evaluating Multiple Ways to Move Projectiles</h3>
        <p>Compare these two ways to move a projectile across the screen:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Method 1 (Translate):</strong> <code>transform.Translate(Vector3.right * speed * Time.deltaTime);</code></li>
            <li><strong>Method 2 (Physics Velocity):</strong> <code>GetComponent&lt;Rigidbody2D&gt;().linearVelocity = direction * speed;</code></li>
        </ul>
        <ol>
            <li>If you want bullets that bounce off stone walls like in a pinball or billiards game, which method (Method 1 or Method 2) is significantly easier, and why?</li>
            <li>If your projectiles are laser beams, energy arrows, or magic missiles that fly straight through obstacles, why is Method 1 clean and lightweight?</li>
            <li>Why is <code>Destroy(gameObject, 5f)</code> called an essential <strong>garbage collection</strong> pattern? What would happen to your game's framerate if a player dodged 1,000 bullets and they all kept flying into deep space forever?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Analyze the Architecture</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Examine how a dungeon dart trap coordinates spawning, aiming, and translational ballistics.
        Notice the two distinct responsibilities: the launcher handles creation, while the projectile
        handles its own orientation, trajectory, and self-destruction:
    </p>

    <pre><code>using UnityEngine;

// Trap launcher coordinating projectile instantiation
public class DartTrap : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private GameObject dartPrefab;
    [SerializeField] private Transform trapMuzzle;

    public void FireDartAt(Vector3 targetCoords)
    {
        if (dartPrefab == null || trapMuzzle == null) return;

        // 1. Spawn projectile clone at muzzle
        GameObject spawnedDart = Instantiate(dartPrefab, trapMuzzle.position, Quaternion.identity);

        // 2. Pass target coordinates to the projectile component
        Dart dartScript = spawnedDart.GetComponent&lt;Dart&gt;();
        if (dartScript != null)
        {
            dartScript.LaunchToward(targetCoords);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>And here is how the projectile manages its orientation and flight:</p>

    <pre><code>using UnityEngine;

// Ballistic dart that rotates to face target and flies forward
public class Dart : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float flightSpeed = 10f;
    [SerializeField] private float autoDestroyTime = 3f;

    public void LaunchToward(Vector3 destination)
    {
        // 1. Calculate normalized direction vector toward destination
        Vector3 flightVector = (destination - transform.position).normalized;

        // 2. Convert vector direction into 2D angle (degrees)
        float rotationAngle = Mathf.Atan2(flightVector.y, flightVector.x) * Mathf.Rad2Deg;
        transform.rotation = Quaternion.AngleAxis(rotationAngle, Vector3.forward);

        // 3. Schedule self-destruction so dodged darts do not leak memory
        Destroy(gameObject, autoDestroyTime);
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        // Fly forward along the rotated local right axis
        transform.Translate(Vector3.right * flightSpeed * Time.deltaTime);
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is <code>transform.rotation</code> calculated using <code>Vector3.forward</code> as the axis of rotation? In 2D math, which axis points toward the camera screen?</li>
            <li>In <code>Dart.Update()</code>, why does <code>Vector3.right</code> fly toward the target? (Hint: How did <code>LaunchToward</code> rotate the transform?)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>Projectile.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>File &amp; Class Name:</strong> In <code>Assets/Scripts/</code>, create a new script named <code>Projectile.cs</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Designer Knobs:</strong> Declare serialized float fields for <code>speed</code> (default <code>6f</code>) and <code>lifetime</code> (default <code>4f</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Initialization Method:</strong> Write a <code>public void Initialize(Vector3 targetPosition)</code> method:
                <ul>
                    <li>Calculate the direction from current position to target: <code>(targetPosition - transform.position).normalized</code>.</li>
                    <li>Compute rotation angle using <code>Mathf.Atan2(direction.y, direction.x) * Mathf.Rad2Deg</code>.</li>
                    <li>Set <code>transform.rotation = Quaternion.AngleAxis(angle, Vector3.forward);</code>.</li>
                    <li>Call <code>Destroy(gameObject, lifetime);</code> to schedule automatic memory cleanup.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Per-Frame Flight in Update:</strong> In <code>Update()</code>, translate the projectile forward along its local right axis using <code>transform.Translate(Vector3.right * speed * Time.deltaTime);</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Prefab Attachment:</strong> Select <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code> and attach your new <code>Projectile</code> component to it.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>TurretController.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Inspector Slots:</strong> Add serialized private fields for <code>GameObject projectilePrefab;</code> and <code>Transform projectileSpawnPoint;</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Attack Method:</strong> Write a private method <code>AttackPlayer()</code>:
                <ul>
                    <li>Guard against null references for <code>projectilePrefab</code> or <code>projectileSpawnPoint</code>.</li>
                    <li>Instantiate the projectile at <code>projectileSpawnPoint.position</code> with <code>Quaternion.identity</code>.</li>
                    <li>Retrieve the <code>Projectile</code> component from the instantiated object and call its <code>Initialize()</code> method, passing <code>playerTransform.position</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Call from Update:</strong> Inside your existing range check (when distance is less than or equal to <code>maxAttackRange</code>), call <code>AttackPlayer();</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Wire Slots in Inspector:</strong> Select the Turret in the Overworld scene. Drag the <code>Turret_Projectile</code> prefab into the projectile slot, and drag the child <code>projectileSpawnPoint</code> into the spawn point slot.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save and press Play. Walk near the turret.
        A projectile spawns from the muzzle, aims at your position, and shoots across the screen!
        (Notice that it fires 60 bullets every second — in lesson 3.3a we fix the cooldown!)
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Projectile Errors</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you leave the <strong>Projectile Prefab</strong> slot unassigned in the Inspector? What error does Unity throw when you step in range?</li>
            <li>In <code>Projectile.cs</code>, what happens if you change <code>Vector3.right</code> to <code>Vector3.up</code> in <code>Update()</code>? Which direction do bullets fly relative to their aim?</li>
            <li>In <code>Projectile.cs</code>, comment out the <code>Destroy(gameObject, lifetime)</code> line. Fire several bullets and watch the Hierarchy in play mode. Do the spawned instances ever leave the scene?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

